#sleela 2.4
<< author-marker-demo.sleela -- the runnable reference for the syntax 2.4
   author/authorial document marker (SLEELA.syntax section 18).

   The bare top-level word `author;` marks this document as handled by
   professional council. Its operational effect: the document may legally
   declare DUPLICATE fields and methods -- each duplicate keeps a distinct
   declaration-order id and an SHA-256 brander, and the standard compile emits
   a second "originated" source document whose members carry a therefore (the
   therefore glyph) brander, the house analogue of Java's @.

   Compile this file to see both outputs:
     sleela compile examples/author-marker-demo.sleela -o /tmp/out.art
   -> writes the artifact AND examples/author-marker-demo.originated.sleela
   A --originate-dir=<dir> flag redirects the originated document. >>

author;

class Greeter {
  String name = "world";

  << Three methods named `greet` -- legal because the document is
     author-marked. Each is branded distinctly in the originated document
     (slot 1/3, 2/3, 3/3). A bare call to a duplicated name currently resolves
     to the last declared definition (see SLEELA.syntax 18.4). >>
  String greet() { return "hello " + name; }
  String greet(String who) { return "hello " + who; }
  String greet(String who, int times) {
    String out = "";
    int i = 0;
    while (i < times) { out = out + "hi " + who + " "; i = i + 1; }
    return out;
  }

  void main() {
    Greeter g = new Greeter();
    print(g.greet("everyone", 2));
    print("done");
  }
}
